WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.061 --> 00:00:01.060
SARA ROBINSON: Hi there.

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:03.101
In this video, I'll show
you some tips and tricks

00:00:03.101 --> 00:00:07.110
to help you prepare your image
data in Cloud AutoML Vision.

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:09.420
Although I'll be showing
AutoML Vision here,

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:11.880
the concepts I'll be showing
apply to AutoML Natural

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:13.672
Language as well.

00:00:13.672 --> 00:00:15.630
For this walkthrough,
we'll be building a model

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:18.000
to detect the type
of cloud in an image,

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.550
because there's no better
place to analyze clouds

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:22.170
than the Cloud.

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:24.810
I've got five possible
labels, each corresponding

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:26.970
with a different type of cloud.

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:29.540
There are many ways to add
labeled images to your AutoML

00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:30.960
Vision data set.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.880
In this video, I'll show
you how to do the following.

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:36.660
First, we'll upload
labeled images directly

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:38.130
from our computer.

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:41.190
Then, we'll review image labels
and add additional images

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:43.170
in the AutoML Vision UI.

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:45.540
The cloud images on my
computer are organized

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:47.280
into folders for each label.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:48.780
Using this approach,
I can create

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:50.280
a zip file of these folders.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:52.460
And when I upload
the zip to AutoML,

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:55.290
it will use the folder names
to automatically assign labels

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:56.580
to each image.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:59.580
For example, this
image of a cirrus cloud

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:01.260
will get the cirrus
label assigned,

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:03.560
since it's in that folder.

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:05.050
I'll create the zip now.

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:10.140
I've already enabled AutoML
Vision for this project

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:11.400
and created my data set.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:15.210
So next, let's upload the
zip file of labeled images.

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:18.480
Note that AutoML Vision has a
30 megabyte file size limit.

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:20.010
For larger data
sets, you can either

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:22.710
create multiple zip files,
or upload your images

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:26.550
to Google Cloud Storage
and create a labeled CSV.

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:28.350
With all of my labeled
images uploaded,

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:31.594
it's time to review them
in the AutoML Vision UI.

00:01:31.594 --> 00:01:33.510
Here, I can look at all
of the images assigned

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:37.140
to a specific label to make
sure they're labeled correctly.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:39.690
Looking at the cirrus images,
it seems like this one

00:01:39.690 --> 00:01:41.610
is incorrectly labeled.

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:44.130
I can click on it and switch
it to the correct label,

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:46.740
cumulonimbus.

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:49.380
Since I created my label
data set, some of my friends

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:50.820
heard about this
project and sent

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:53.880
over a few of their own cloud
images to be used in training.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:55.080
Let's add them.

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:58.317
From the All Images tab, I can
select Add Images, and then

00:01:58.317 --> 00:01:59.400
Upload From Your Computer.

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:07.260
Notice that because I uploaded
these from the All Images tab,

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:09.660
they went into the
Unlabeled category.

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:12.024
I can click on Unlabelled
to go in and add labels

00:02:12.024 --> 00:02:12.690
to these images.

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:17.900
One of my friends
is a cloud expert

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:21.080
and sent me images
specifically of cirrus clouds.

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:24.500
I can apply that label to them
directly by clicking on Cirrus,

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:26.720
and then adding the images.

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:28.760
Now that I've got all
of my images uploaded,

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:32.300
let's explore what I can do
with the image selection tool.

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:35.940
I can click the check mark here
to select all images on a page,

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:37.580
or I can select
individual images

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:40.730
by clicking on the checkbox
on the top left of an image.

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:44.000
With images selected, I can move
them to the Unlabeled category

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:47.419
by clicking on Label,
and then Unlabel.

00:02:47.419 --> 00:02:48.960
These images will
still be in my data

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:50.750
set and will remain
in Cloud storage,

00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:52.250
but because they
don't have a label,

00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:53.720
they won't be used in training.

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:55.880
To delete an image from
my data set entirely

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:58.700
and from Google Cloud Storage,
I can select it, and then

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:00.830
press Delete.

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:03.950
There are also some handy label
ordering tools on the left.

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:05.720
If I click on the
menu bar here, I

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:07.820
can choose how I want
to order my labels.

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:10.190
I can order them
alphabetically, or if I

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:12.440
want to see where I
need to add more images,

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:14.730
I can sort from fewest to most.

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:17.030
Remember that you need at
least 10 images per label

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:19.250
to train a model
in AutoML Vision.

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:21.830
To start gathering images
of a new cloud type,

00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:24.770
I can add the label here and
start importing images for it.

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:30.410
Now that I've
uploaded all my images

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:31.970
and labeled them
correctly, I can

00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:36.230
export the data set to a
cloud storage directory.

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:38.600
This will create a
CSV file with details

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:41.030
on whether an image was
included in the training, test,

00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:44.600
or validation data set, its
Google Cloud Storage URL,

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:47.164
and its corresponding label.

00:03:47.164 --> 00:03:49.580
This is useful if I want to
share the data set with others

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:51.590
on my team, or see
how my data set was

00:03:51.590 --> 00:03:53.780
split for training and testing.

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:55.580
With all my images
uploaded and labeled,

00:03:55.580 --> 00:03:57.140
I'm ready to train my model.

00:03:57.140 --> 00:03:58.940
The great thing about
AutoML is that I

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:00.314
don't need to
worry about writing

00:04:00.314 --> 00:04:01.910
any of the code for my model.

00:04:01.910 --> 00:04:05.100
All I need to do is click
a button to train it.

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:06.110
That's all for now.

00:04:06.110 --> 00:04:10.430
Stay tuned for future videos
to cover more AutoML features.

